  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 240 PM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry, seasonable weather for Halloween weekend\ncontinues into early next week. \n\nEarly afternoon wx analysis shows ~1028mb high pressure centered\nover the upper Great Lakes, ridging SE toward the local area. The\nwell-advertised dry cold front drops across the region over the\nnext few hours, with winds becoming N-NW behind the frontal\npassage. No real cold air advection just yet...allowing for one\nmore warm day across the region. 18z Obs were well into the 70s\nover the area, with some readings in the lower 80s across the\npiedmont. \n\nCAA arrives tonight, with winds increasing and veering around\nthe N then NNE tonight and overnight. Temps fall into the 40s \nin most areas, with lower 50s in coastal SE VA/NE NC with the \nflow off the water. Cooler, dry, and pleasant on Halloween, with\nhighs in the lower to middle 60s. Given the cool, dry airmass\nand diminishing winds, temps fall off quickly (to the upper \n40s-50s) during the evening. High pressure builds over the area\ntomorrow night through Sunday. Lows Sunday morning drop into \nthe low-mid 40s by Sunday morning, with even cooler readings\npossible Monday morning. Turning slightly milder (upper 60s) on\nSunday with some additional clouds Sunday morning ahead of an \nweakening shortwave approaching from the SW. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain chances have continued to trend down for early \nnext week, with no precipitation currently in the forecast. Rain \nchances may return by Thursday with above average temperatures to \nstart the month of November.\n\nThe chance for measurable rainfall continues to decrease for Sunday \nnight into Monday despite the passage of the previously\nreferenced shortwave. Despite considerable cloudiness, low-level\nmoisture will be significantly lacking. Looking ahead, a gradual\nwarming trend takes place early next week, as low amplitude \nupper ridging builds east across the region, as surface high\npressure moves well offshore. Widespread lower-mid 70s appear \nlikely on both days. Precipitation chances finally return to the\nforecast by next Thursday-Thursday night as a stronger low \npressure system potentially tracks from the Mississippi River \nValley to the Mid-Atlantic/Northeast. However, forecast \nconfidence in this potential welcome rainfall remains \nunderstandably low given the timing.",
